# Travel, the land and the turning year (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, phase 7): chains. One asks where the other grew up,
# or whether they've ever been far, or what the country round here is good for, or says the season's turning; the
# other answers as they are (where they grew up and where they stand now, their work, their age, their purse, their
# temper), and the first answers that in turn. Every beat is the speaker's own best rule, so a question has dozens of
# answers.
#
# Chains (reviewed 2026-10-13):
#   where are you from:   talk.travel -> travel.from (remembers tf=home|away) -> travel.from_reply
#                         -> (away) travel.home: what it's like there -> tale.react
#   ever been far:        talk.travel -> travel.been -> tale.react (a tale) | travel.longing (a wish) | (nothing)
#   the road:             talk.travel -> road.react (a worry or a hard road) | travel.longing (wanting to be off)
#   the country:          talk.land -> land.answer -> land.react
#   the turning year:     talk.season -> season.answer
# The map is doc 60's: the Southway runs from Ridgemere's South Gate to Oakhaven, the Fen Road on to Mirebridge, the
# High Road from the East Gate to Coldwater, the Pass Road up to Frosthollow, the Downs Road from Coldwater to
# Larkfield, the Riverway from Oakhaven to Larkfield, the South Road to Southmeadow, and the Meadow Road from
# Mirebridge across the grain country to Southmeadow.

# ===================================================================================== Where are you from?

rule e5_ask_from_stranger
when concept=talk.travel pair.band=strangers
cooldown 1d
say Where do you hail from, #address#? | I don't know your face. Where's home for you? | I can't place your voice. Where are you from? | You'll forgive me asking. Where did you grow up?
then other: travel.from

rule e5_ask_from_acquaintance
when concept=talk.travel pair.band=acquaintances|rivals
cooldown 2d
say I never asked: where did you grow up? | All the times we've talked, and I don't know where you were born. | Where's your family from, then? Originally.
then other: travel.from

rule e5_ask_from_chatty
when concept=talk.travel self.talk=chatty pair.band=strangers|acquaintances
cooldown 2d
say Now settle something for me. Where were you born? I've a bet on with myself. | I'm guessing... no, you tell me. Where are you from? | I've been trying to work out your voice all this while. Where's home?
then other: travel.from

# Answering, as they are (self answers). At home:
rule e5_from_home_ridgemere
when concept=travel.from self.from=ridgemere here.region=ridgemere
say Here. Born under the rain, like everyone. | Ridgemere, born and bred. | Here. I've never lived anywhere else, and my fur's never been properly dry. | Here, in the city. Three streets from where I'm standing.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_frosthollow
when concept=travel.from self.from=frosthollow here.region=frosthollow
say Here. Up the pass, where the snow lives. | Frosthollow. My father dug, his father dug. | Here, under the Whitefangs. Born in a snowdrift, #mum# says. | Here. Never been further than the bottom of the Pass Road.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_coldwater
when concept=travel.from self.from=coldwater here.region=coldwater
say Here, on the lake. I could row before I could walk. | Coldwater. The lake's in my blood, cold as it is. | Born in a boat-shed, three doors down. | Here. You can see the house from the shore, the one with the nets on the wall.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_oakhaven
when concept=travel.from self.from=oakhaven here.region=oakhaven
say Here, in the Warmwood. Under the oaks. | Oakhaven, and my grandmother before me. | Here. I don't think I'd know what to do with a sky that had no leaves in it. | Here. Born the year the sawmill burned, they tell me.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_mirebridge
when concept=travel.from self.from=mirebridge here.region=mirebridge
say Here. Fen-born, fen-bred, damp all through. | Mirebridge. Where else? | Out on the causeway, the house with the eel traps. | Here. My people have cut reed in these fens longer than anyone's kept count.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_larkfield
when concept=travel.from self.from=larkfield here.region=larkfield
say Here, on the downs. Lambs and larks. | Larkfield. Born on a Marketday, so they say I came out haggling. | Here. Up past the mill, where the chalk shows. | Here. Never had a reason to leave, with every road coming to us.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

rule e5_from_home_southmeadow
when concept=travel.from self.from=southmeadow here.region=southmeadow
say Here, among the apples. | Southmeadow. Born at harvest, under a hay cart. | Here. Why would anyone be from anywhere else? | Here. #mum# still keeps the orchard out past the Cider House.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=home 2m

# Grown elsewhere:
rule e5_from_away_ridgemere
when concept=travel.from self.from=ridgemere here.region!=ridgemere
say Ridgemere. The city. Don't hold it against me. | The city, down by the Sound. I still dream of rain. | Ridgemere. You can tell, can't you? It's the airs. | The city. Born in the Commons, a stone's throw from the Black Tun.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_frosthollow
when concept=travel.from self.from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow
say Frosthollow. Up in the snow. | The mining town, up the Pass Road. It's warm down here. Too warm. | Frosthollow. Up under the Whitefangs, where the road gives up. | Frosthollow. You'd never get me back up that pass in winter.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_coldwater
when concept=travel.from self.from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater
say Coldwater, on Glasswater. Lake folk. | Coldwater. I still miss the water. | The lake, under the mountains. Coldwater. | Coldwater, where the High Road starts to climb. Fishing folk, all of us.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_oakhaven
when concept=travel.from self.from=oakhaven here.region!=oakhaven
say Oakhaven, in the Warmwood. | The woods. Oakhaven. Too much sky out here for me. | Oakhaven. I grew up under the oaks. | Oakhaven. Sawdust in my fur from the day I was born.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_mirebridge
when concept=travel.from self.from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge
say Mirebridge, in the fens. Go on, say it. | The Sallow Fens. Mirebridge. | Mirebridge. Eels and mist, and proud of it. | Down the Long River, in the fens. Mirebridge.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_larkfield
when concept=travel.from self.from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield
say Larkfield, on the downs. | The downs. Larkfield. Sheep country. | Larkfield. Market town. I can still smell the fleeces. | Larkfield, where the Lark runs down off the downs.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

rule e5_from_away_southmeadow
when concept=travel.from self.from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow
say Southmeadow, down south. | Southmeadow. The orchards. I miss the sun. | The far south, among the apples. Southmeadow. | Southmeadow. As far south as the roads go.
then other: travel.from_reply
remember pair: tf=away 2m

# The first answers that (self is the first again). From the same place: the best, whoever they are.
rule e5_from_reply_same
when concept=travel.from_reply pair.same_from=yes
priority 2
say Same as me! Then we're near enough cousins. | Never! Which street? We'll know someone. | I knew it. I could hear it the moment you spoke. | Same here. Small realm, isn't it? | Get away. So am I. Who are your people?

# Born where they stand (the asker isn't):
rule e5_from_reply_local
when concept=travel.from_reply pair.mem.tf=home pair.same_from=no
say Born here. I envy you that. | Then you'll know all the short cuts. I'm still finding them. | A local. Then I'll ask you where the decent ale is. | Born and bred, eh? You'll have seen it change, then.

# A fallback, should the memory be missing: fits both.
rule e5_from_reply
when concept=travel.from_reply
say Is that so. | Ah. I'd not have guessed. | There's worse places to come from. | Well, we all have to be from somewhere.

# Grown somewhere else than here: a word about the place, which they answer (travel.home).
rule e5_from_reply_ridgemere
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=ridgemere pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say The city! Do you miss the rain? Folk say you can't get it out of your fur. | Ridgemere. Did you ever get up on the Heights, among the fine folk? | A city wolf. What's it like, living cheek by jowl with that many? | Ridgemere. I've only ever seen the walls from the road. What's it like inside?
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_frosthollow
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=frosthollow pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Frosthollow! No wonder you never feel the cold. | Up the Pass Road? What's it like, living in the snow? | Miners' country. Hard folk, they say. | Is it true the snow comes up to the eaves?
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_coldwater
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=coldwater pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Glasswater! Is it as clear as they say? | Coldwater. That's where the High Road starts climbing, isn't it? | Lake folk. I'd never trust a boat myself. | Coldwater. What do you all do in winter, when the lake freezes?
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_oakhaven
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=oakhaven pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Oakhaven? In the Warmwood? I'd lose myself in an hour. | Woods folk. Is it true the old oaks are older than the realm? | All those trees. Doesn't it get dark under them? | Oakhaven. Half the tables in the realm came out of your sawmill, I hear.
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_mirebridge
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=mirebridge pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Mirebridge! Damp country, I hear. | I've heard about the lights out on the fens at night. | Eel country. I've never been brave enough to eat one. | Mirebridge. How do you build anything on a bog?
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_larkfield
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=larkfield pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Larkfield. Sheep, and more sheep, I hear. | The downs! All that sky. | Market folk. I'll mind my purse, then. | Larkfield. That's where all the roads meet, isn't it?
then other: travel.home

rule e5_from_reply_southmeadow
when concept=travel.from_reply other.from=southmeadow pair.mem.tf=away pair.same_from=no
say Southmeadow! Why ever did you leave? Sun and apples. | The far south. Easy living, I hear. | Cider country. Is the Cider House as good as they say? | All that sun, and you came up here?
then other: travel.home

# What it's like there (self, grown elsewhere, answers). Each fits every word the other might have said about it.
rule e5_home_ridgemere
when concept=travel.home self.from=ridgemere
say Wet, loud and steep. You'd hate it for a month and miss it ever after. | Rain most days, fog the rest. But the wharf at dawn, with the boats going out... | Too many wolves and too many stairs. I still miss the Commons Market of a Marketday. | Busy. Walk from the Sump to the Heights and you'll not hear the same voice twice.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_frosthollow
when concept=travel.home self.from=frosthollow
say Cold enough to crack a kettle. But the stars up there. You've never seen stars. | Snow to the eaves in a bad winter, aye. You dig out and get on. | Folk think we're hard. We're not. The rock's hard; we're only stubborn. | Dark half the year, and the whole valley smells of the smelter. I'd go back tomorrow.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_coldwater
when concept=travel.home self.from=coldwater
say The lake's so clear you can count the stones on the bottom, and cold enough to stop your heart. | Quiet. Boats at dawn, nets in the afternoon, and the mountains standing over everything. | Everyone bound up the Pass Road stops a night with us, so you hear all the news. Fish and news. | In winter we cut holes in the ice, sit by them, and lie to each other. Best time of year.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_oakhaven
when concept=travel.home self.from=oakhaven
say Green, mostly. Green light, green shade. You stop missing the sky. | Quiet as a chapel under the old oaks. Then the sawmill starts and you can't hear yourself think. | You learn the paths as a pup and never get lost. Strangers do. Every summer we go and fetch one out. | Warm and dim and smelling of leaf mould and sawdust. I miss it more than I let on.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_mirebridge
when concept=travel.home self.from=mirebridge
say Damp, aye. But the light on the water at dusk, and the reeds whispering. You don't get that on dry ground. | Folk say it's all bog and lights and eels. It is. That's what's good about it. | Everything stands on piles and causeways, and nothing's quite level. You walk with a lean for a month after. | Mist every morning and frogs every night, and folk who mind their own business. I liked that.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_larkfield
when concept=travel.home self.from=larkfield
say Wind and sheep and the mill wheel going. You can see weather a day before it gets to you. | Every road crosses there sooner or later, so there's always someone new at the inn, and always someone selling. | Open country. Chalk under the grass and larks over it. I can't sleep under trees. | Lambs in spring, fleeces in summer, the butcher run off his paws all autumn. You always know the time of year.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_home_southmeadow
when concept=travel.home self.from=southmeadow
say Easy? Try picking apples from dawn till dusk in that heat. But aye, it's lovely. | Orchards to the skyline, the Cider House on the corner, and the Press clattering away. I do miss it. | Warm and slow, and everyone's somebody's cousin. That's why I left, and why I'll go back. | Gold fields and full barns when the rain comes. When it doesn't, you've never seen such worried wolves.
then other: tale.react

# ===================================================================================== Ever been far?

rule e5_ask_been
when concept=talk.travel
cooldown 2d
say Ever been far from here? | Where's the furthest you've ever been? | Have you travelled much, #address#? | Ever been on the road? Properly, I mean.
then other: travel.been

rule e5_ask_been_close
when concept=talk.travel pair.band=friends|family|spouses
cooldown 2d
say You've been about a bit, haven't you? Tell me again. | I never did hear where you went, that time. | What's the furthest you've ever been, #pet#? I've forgotten.
then other: travel.been

# Answering (self answers). Those who came from elsewhere:
rule e5_been_moved_ridgemere
when concept=travel.been self.from=ridgemere here.region!=ridgemere
say Only here, from the city. That was far enough. The quiet nearly killed me. | Left Ridgemere with one bag. Never went back for the rest. | From the Sound to here. I still look for the sea of a morning.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_frosthollow
when concept=travel.been self.from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow
say Down the Pass Road with my pack, and here I stopped. | I came down out of the snow one spring and kept walking till it was warm. | Down the pass, past Glasswater, and on. Every mile I took off a layer.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_coldwater
when concept=travel.been self.from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater
say Off the lake and down here. I don't know why. Love, I think. | Coldwater to here. A week and a ruined pair of boots. | I rowed half my life. Then I walked. You can't row everywhere.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_oakhaven
when concept=travel.been self.from=oakhaven here.region!=oakhaven
say Out of the Warmwood and onto the open road. First time I'd seen a proper horizon. | From Oakhaven. Took me a month to stop ducking under branches that weren't there. | I left the woods for work. The trees didn't miss me.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_mirebridge
when concept=travel.been self.from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge
say Out of the fens. Took me three days to stop squelching. | Mirebridge to here, on the back of an eel cart. | I left the fens for dry ground. Still not used to it.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_larkfield
when concept=travel.been self.from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield
say Off the downs with a drover's herd, and I never went back. | From Larkfield. Half the roads in the realm meet there, so I took one. | Larkfield to here. I missed the wind for a year.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_moved_southmeadow
when concept=travel.been self.from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow
say Up from the south. Every mile colder than the last. | From Southmeadow. Came for a sweetheart. The sweetheart went; I stayed. | Up out of the orchards, and the sun got smaller every day.
then other: tale.react

# By what they do and are:
rule e5_been_carter
when concept=travel.been self.job=carter
say Every road in the realm, and every rut in them. | The Southway, the High Road, the Downs Road. Name one I haven't. | Too far, too often. My back knows every pothole between here and Southmeadow.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_sailor
when concept=travel.been self.job=sailor
say Round the headland and down the coast, more times than I can count. | Out past the Sound where the water goes black. You don't come back the same. | Down the coast to where the Long River comes out through the Drowned Delta. Never again.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_merchant
when concept=travel.been self.role=merchant
say Larkfield, the city, the towns. Where the coin goes, I go. | Far enough to know the prices are better somewhere else. | Every town with a road to it. Buy in one, sell in the next. That's the trade.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_soldier
when concept=travel.been self.job=soldier|guard
say Marched the Southway twice. My feet still remember. | Escorted a caravan up the Pass Road once. Never again. | Wherever I was sent. You don't ask.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_priest
when concept=travel.been self.job=priest
say I walked up to the Snow Chapel at Frosthollow once, as a novice. Barefoot, as was the custom. | A pilgrimage, when I was young. The road is a prayer, they say. My paws said otherwise. | Where the Bright One sent me. Here, mostly.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_young
when concept=travel.been self.stage=young
say Not past the gate! #mum# won't let me. | I went to the mill once. That's far. | Not yet. But I will. All the way to the end of the road!

rule e5_been_old
when concept=travel.been self.stage=old
say Once, when I was young and foolish. Didn't think much of it. | Far enough to know home's best. | Not any more. My knees did the deciding.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_old_broad
when concept=travel.been self.stage=old self.accent=broad
say Never. Never needed to. Everything I want's in sight of my door. | Been to the next town, once. For a funeral. Didn't care for it. | Why would I? I'd only have to come back.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_rich
when concept=travel.been self.wealth=rich
say Ridgemere for the Council season, of course, and the country after. | Here and there. One must keep an eye on one's interests. | Oh, everywhere worth going. Which isn't many places.
then other: tale.react

rule e5_been_poor
when concept=travel.been self.wealth=poor|destitute
say On what? Travelling's for those with coin. | Furthest I've been is the pawnshop. | My paws carry me to work and back. That's all the road I can afford.

rule e5_been_restless
when concept=travel.been self.drive=restless
say Not far enough. Never far enough. | Not yet. I lie awake and plan it, though. | One day I'll just go. Down the road and not stop.
then other: travel.longing

rule e5_been_content
when concept=travel.been self.drive=content
say Why would I? Everything I want's in walking distance. | No. I've a chair, a fire, and a view. That'll do. | Here's fine. Here's always been fine.

rule e5_been_nervy
when concept=travel.been self.temper=nervy
say #oath#, no. Bandits, wolves, #mud#. No. | I went as far as the toll gate once and had to sit down. | The road's full of strangers. No thank you.

rule e5_been_plain
when concept=travel.been
say A wedding in the next town, once. Danced all night, walked home all day. | Far enough. Once. Came home with blisters and a cold. | I had a cousin who went everywhere. I went with him as far as the first inn. | Out to the coast once, to see the sea. It was grey. I'd hoped for more.
then other: tale.react

# ===================================================================================== Hearing a tale (of a journey, or a home far off)

rule e5_react
when concept=tale.react
say Is that so. | Fancy that. | Huh. I never knew that. | Well, well. | You don't hear that every day.

rule e5_react_warm
when concept=tale.react self.warmth=warm
say I'd have loved to see that. | You make me want to see it for myself. | You tell it well. I can almost see it.

rule e5_react_cold
when concept=tale.react self.warmth=cold
say If you say so. | Hm. | And?

rule e5_react_dry
when concept=tale.react self.humour=dry
say Fascinating. Truly. I'll treasure it. | Gripping. Is there more, or may I go? | I'll be sure to tell my grandchildren. Twice.

rule e5_react_merry
when concept=tale.react self.humour=merry
say Ha! Tell that in the tavern and they'll stand you ale all night. | Ha! I'd pay good coin to have seen that. | You do spin a tale. Go on, what happened then?

rule e5_react_restless
when concept=tale.react self.drive=restless
say #oath#, that makes me want to be off somewhere. | Don't. You're making me itch to leave. | Next time, take me. I mean it.

rule e5_react_nervy
when concept=tale.react self.temper=nervy
say You wouldn't get me out there. | My fur's standing up just hearing it. | Rather you than me.

rule e5_react_quiet
when concept=tale.react self.talk=quiet
say Mm. | Huh. | Hm. Fancy.

rule e5_react_young
when concept=tale.react self.stage=young
say Take me next time! | Really? Really really? | That's the best thing I ever heard.

rule e5_react_old
when concept=tale.react self.stage=old
say I knew a wolf who talked like that. Never did settle. | Things were different when I was young. Worse, mostly. | Aye. The world's wide and home's narrow. Both have their uses.

rule e5_react_guarded
when concept=tale.react self.honesty=guarded|sly
say Is that so. Is that so. | Funny how every traveller's tale grows a little each telling. | I'll believe half.

rule e5_react_devout
when concept=tale.react self.piety=devout
say The Bright One's hand is in all of it. | Give thanks for it, then. | Every road's a prayer, they say, and every field.

# ===================================================================================== Wanting to be off (travel.longing)

rule e5_longing
when concept=travel.longing
say Then go. Who's holding you? | The road'll still be there next year. So will you, I expect. | Everyone says that. Hardly anyone goes. | Send word if you do. I'd like to know what's out there.

rule e5_longing_close
when concept=travel.longing pair.band=friends|family|spouses
say And leave me here with nobody to talk to? | You say that every spring, #pet#. | Go on, then. I'll keep your chair warm.

rule e5_longing_content
when concept=travel.longing self.drive=content
say Not me. I'd miss my own bed. | You go. I'll mind the fire. | I went once. Came back the same, only footsore.

rule e5_longing_restless
when concept=travel.longing self.drive=restless
say Take me with you. I mean it. | Every morning I think it. Every evening I'm still here. | The day you go, knock on my door.

rule e5_longing_old
when concept=travel.longing self.stage=old
say Go while your legs are good. Mine aren't, now. | I said that once. Then I blinked, and here I am. | The road waits for nobody. Mind that.

rule e5_longing_young
when concept=travel.longing self.stage=young
say Can I come? I'll carry things! | Where? Where will you go? | I'm going too, when I'm big.

rule e5_longing_nervy
when concept=travel.longing self.temper=nervy
say Out there? With #bandits# and #mud# and strangers? | Don't even say it. You'd be robbed by the first milestone. | I'd worry myself sick.

# ===================================================================================== The road (talk.travel, one speaker on)

rule e5_road_bandits
when concept=talk.travel world.bandits=near
priority 1
cooldown 1d
say Nobody sensible's on the roads this week. Not with #bandits# about. | #bandits.capitalize# on the road again. My cousin's waiting till they're gone. | I was going to visit my sister. Not now. Not with them out there.
then other: road.react

rule e5_road_carter_complaint
when concept=talk.travel self.job=carter
cooldown 1d
say Three days on the Downs Road and two of them in the #mud#. | Lost a wheel on the Fen Road again. Third this year. | The inns on the High Road charge like they own the mountain.
then other: road.react

rule e5_road_restless_wish
when concept=talk.travel self.drive=restless
cooldown 2d
say One of these mornings I'm just going to walk out the gate and keep going. | Every cart that rolls past, I want to be up on the back of it. | Where does the road go after the last town? Has anyone ever found out?
then other: travel.longing

rule e5_road_travelling
when concept=talk.travel self.travelling=yes
cooldown 1d
say On the road again. I've slept in more strange beds this month than all last year. | I'm away again in the morning. My paws haven't forgiven me for the last stretch. | Another day's walking tomorrow. I'll be glad of my own bed when I see it.
then other: road.react

# The answer to a hard road (self answers).
rule e5_roadr
when concept=road.react
say Mind how you go, that's all. | The roads have always been hard. They'll outlast the lot of us. | Better the long way round than the short way to trouble. | Rather you than me.

rule e5_roadr_carter
when concept=road.react self.job=carter
say Don't. I'm out on the Riverway myself in the morning. | Same on every road. Ruts, tolls and bad beer. | You learn to sleep on the box. That's the only trick there is.

rule e5_roadr_guard
when concept=road.react self.job=guard|soldier
say Keep to the main road and move by day. That's my advice, for what it's worth. | We'd ride further out if the Council paid for it. | If you see anything, you come and tell the watch post. Anything.

rule e5_roadr_merchant
when concept=road.react self.role=merchant
say And every hard mile goes on the price. Mark me. | That's my goods late again, then. | The roads cost me more than my rent, some months.

rule e5_roadr_nervy
when concept=road.react self.temper=nervy
say Stop. My fur's on end already. | That's why I stay home. That, exactly. | You be careful. Promise me.

rule e5_roadr_dry
when concept=road.react self.humour=dry
say The roads of this realm are a penance. I've decided to enjoy mine. | And yet folk still ask why I never visit. | Lovely. Write me when you get there. If you get there.

rule e5_roadr_young
when concept=road.react self.stage=young
say Is it scary, out on the road? | I'd go. I'm not scared. | Did you see any #bandits#? Did you?

rule e5_roadr_warm
when concept=road.react self.warmth=warm
say Come and eat with us before you set off. You'll want something warm in you. | Stay safe, #address#. I mean that. | I'll keep a light in the window, for luck.

# ===================================================================================== The country round here (talk.land)

rule e5_land_ask
when concept=talk.land
cooldown 2d
say What do you make of the land round here? | Is it good ground, round here? I can never tell. | You know this country. What's it good for? | What's out past the last houses, anyway? I've never looked.
then other: land.answer

rule e5_land_ask_stranger
when concept=talk.land pair.band=strangers
cooldown 1d
say I'm new to this country. What's it like? | Anything I should know about the country round here? | Fine country. Is it as good as it looks?
then other: land.answer

rule e5_land_view
when concept=talk.land here.time=day
cooldown 2d
say Look at it all out there. Do you ever stop and look? | Some days you could almost love the place. | Stand here a moment. Just look at it. What do you see?
then other: land.answer

# By where they stand (self answers):
rule e5_land_ridgemere
when concept=land.answer here.region=ridgemere
say Wet. Steep. Lovely, if you like moss. | The Rainward Hills behind us, the Sound in front, and rain in between. | The Rainwood's thick as fur up past the Houses' lands. Folk get lost in it. | Every street's a hill and every hill's a stream.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_frosthollow
when concept=land.answer here.region=frosthollow
say Rock and snow and pines. Good for ore, bad for cabbages. | The mountain gives, and the mountain takes. Mostly takes. | Up here the land's mostly sky and stone. | Short summer, long winter, and a view to stop your heart.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_coldwater
when concept=land.answer here.region=coldwater
say Water. The lake, the streams, the snowmelt. Everything here is water. | Glasswater on one side, the mountains on the other, and us squeezed between. | Stony fields, cold water, and the best trout in the realm. Fair trade.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_oakhaven
when concept=land.answer here.region=oakhaven
say Trees. Then more trees. Then a clearing, and more trees. | The Warmwood. It gives you everything, if you know where to look. | The oaks are older than the town. Older than the realm, some say.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_mirebridge
when concept=land.answer here.region=mirebridge
say Half of it's water and the other half's lying about it. | The fens. Step wrong and they keep your boot. | Peat, reed and eel. Rich, if you can stand the damp. | Flat as a plate and twice as wet.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_larkfield
when concept=land.answer here.region=larkfield
say Grass and chalk and sky. Sheep think it's heaven. | The downs roll on forever. You can see weather coming a day off. | Thin soil, sweet grass. Good for wool, hard on ploughs.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_southmeadow
when concept=land.answer here.region=southmeadow
say Best soil in the realm. Shame about the rain, or the lack of it. | Grain to the horizon, apples in the vale. Hard to go hungry here. | Warm and gold and flat. You could see a wagon coming from half a day off.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_wild
when concept=land.answer here.region=wild
say Out here? It belongs to nobody, and it knows it. | Wild ground. Keep to the road. | No fences, no fields. Just the land minding its own business.
then other: land.react

# By their work (self answers):
rule e5_land_farmer
when concept=land.answer self.job=farmer
say Depends which field. The low one's clay, the top one's stones. | Good enough, if you break your back for it. | The soil's fine. It's the weather that's the liar. | Give me a dry week and a sharp plough and I'll tell you.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_woodcutter
when concept=land.answer self.job=woodcutter
say I see trees, I see timber. Can't help it. | There's a stand of old oak past the brook that'd make a fortune. Not mine to cut. | The woods are thinning on the near side. Too many of us.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_miner
when concept=land.answer self.job=miner
say Every hill's got something in it. Question is how deep. | I don't look at the land. I look under it. | Rock's honest. Grass lies about what's under it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_fisher
when concept=land.answer self.job=fisher
say Land's just the bit between the water. | I couldn't tell you. I'm always looking the other way, at the water. | Good banks, poor banks. That's all land is to me.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_herder
when concept=land.answer self.job=herder
say Good grazing on the slopes, sour in the bottoms. Ask any ewe. | The beasts know it better than I do. I just follow them. | There's a spring up top that never fails. Worth more than gold, that.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_carter
when concept=land.answer self.job=carter
say Ruts. I know every rut in it. | Too many hills and not enough bridges. | Land's fine. It's the roads across it that are the trouble.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_priest
when concept=land.answer self.job=priest
say The Bright One made it. It's not for us to grumble at it. | Every field's a sermon, if you listen. | Bountiful, when we're grateful for it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_young
when concept=land.answer self.stage=young
say There's a den out past the fields! Don't tell anyone. | There's a stream where you can catch frogs. And a tree you can climb right up. | It's big. Is it all ours?
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_rich
when concept=land.answer self.wealth=rich
say Good investment, land. It doesn't run off. | I prefer it from a carriage window. | Pretty enough. I leave the mud to those who're paid for it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_laborer
when concept=land.answer self.job=laborer
say Heavy. All of it. I've carried most. | I've dug it, hauled it and cursed it. Fine land. | Every ditch round here, I dug. Ask me about ditches.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_plain
when concept=land.answer
say Good enough for us. | It's home. That's all I need it to be. | Pretty, when the light's right. | I don't think about it much. I suppose I should.
then other: land.react

# Their work where they are (self answers; these win, so they rest a day):
rule e5_land_farmer_southmeadow
when concept=land.answer self.job=farmer here.region=southmeadow
cooldown 1d
say Black loam two spades deep. Push a stick in and it sprouts. | The Sunfields would feed the realm if the sky would only rain. | Rich ground. Thirsty ground. We carry water half the summer.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_farmer_larkfield
when concept=land.answer self.job=farmer here.region=larkfield
cooldown 1d
say Chalk an inch down. Every furrow turns up flints. | Thin. You plough round the flints and pray. | The bottom fields by the Lark are good. The rest are for sheep.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_farmer_mirebridge
when concept=land.answer self.job=farmer here.region=mirebridge
cooldown 1d
say Black and wet. Dig a ditch and it fills before supper. | The peat grows anything, if you can keep the water off. | We farm on stilts, near enough.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_farmer_oakhaven
when concept=land.answer self.job=farmer here.region=oakhaven
cooldown 1d
say Every field here was forest once. The roots still fight the plough. | Leaf mould, good and soft. But the shade steals the sun. | We grubbed out stumps for three winters to make that field.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_herder_larkfield
when concept=land.answer self.job=herder here.region=larkfield
cooldown 1d
say Best grazing in the realm, the high downs. Sheep up there never want for anything. | The sheep get fat and the shepherd gets wind-burnt. Fair trade. | The fold on the top is older than the town. The grass knows it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_herder_ridgemere
when concept=land.answer self.job=herder here.region=ridgemere
cooldown 1d
say The Folds are wet but green. Sheep with damp feet, rabbits everywhere. | Grass grows quick in the rain. So does foot rot. | The Folds are good to beasts. Bad to the wolves who mind them.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_miner_frosthollow
when concept=land.answer self.job=miner here.region=frosthollow
cooldown 1d
say The Whitefangs are full of iron. You just have to ask nicely, with a pick. | There's a seam in the high mine runs like a vein in a wrist. | The mountain's generous. It's also patient. It'll have us all back one day.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_miner_ridgemere
when concept=land.answer self.job=miner here.region=ridgemere
cooldown 1d
say The Rainward Hills are honeycombed with Grayrock's tunnels. Wet ones. | Good stone, poor ore. And water in every shaft. | Grayrock's mine goes deeper every year. The hill doesn't like it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_fisher_coldwater
when concept=land.answer self.job=fisher here.region=coldwater
cooldown 1d
say Glasswater's deep as the mountains are high. Nobody's found the bottom. | Clear as air, and cold enough to stop your heart. The trout love it. | The lake's kind in summer. In winter it's a sheet of iron, and you fish through it.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_fisher_ridgemere
when concept=land.answer self.job=fisher|sailor here.region=ridgemere
cooldown 1d
say The Sound's kind inside the headland and cruel past it. | Seals on the rocks, herring in the bay, and fog whenever it likes. | The tide out past the headland could take a boat anywhere. It's taken a few.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_fisher_mirebridge
when concept=land.answer self.job=fisher here.region=mirebridge
cooldown 1d
say The channels shift every spring. Learn them again or lose your boat. | The Long River slows here and goes lazy. The eels love it. | Down past the town it's all delta. Water every way you look.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_woodcutter_oakhaven
when concept=land.answer self.job=woodcutter here.region=oakhaven
cooldown 1d
say The Warmwood grows back faster than we cut it. Nearly. | Oak for beams, beech for the fire, hazel for everything else. | There's a stand of oaks past the second clearing nobody cuts. Bad luck. Don't ask why.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_woodcutter_frosthollow
when concept=land.answer self.job=woodcutter here.region=frosthollow
cooldown 1d
say Pine, pine and more pine. It burns hot and spits. | The pines grow slow up here. Cut one and you've cut a hundred winters. | We only fell in winter, when the sap's down and the sledges run.
then other: land.react

rule e5_land_woodcutter_ridgemere
when concept=land.answer self.job=woodcutter here.region=ridgemere
cooldown 1d
say The Rainwood's cedar. Smells like a chapel when you cut it. | Fell's camps have most of the Rainwood. We just swing the axes. | Moss on everything. Even the axe handles grow it if you leave them.
then other: land.react

# What the asker makes of it (self is the asker again).
rule e5_landr
when concept=land.react
say I'll take your word for it. | Funny. I'd never looked at it that way. | You'd know better than me. | That's more than I knew this morning.

rule e5_landr_stranger
when concept=land.react pair.band=strangers
say Much obliged. I'll know where I'm putting my paws, now. | Good to know. Thank you, #address#. | I'll remember that.

rule e5_landr_farmer
when concept=land.react self.job=farmer
say Any ground'll feed you if you work it hard enough. | Hm. I'd want to see the soil before I believed it. | Good. Ground's worth more than gold, in the end.

rule e5_landr_dry
when concept=land.react self.humour=dry
say You should sell that to travellers. They'd pay for it. | Beautifully put. I've gone quite misty. | And to think I came out for a quiet walk.

rule e5_landr_young
when concept=land.react self.stage=young
say Can we go and look? Now? | I'm going to see all of it. Every bit. | Is there treasure in it?

rule e5_landr_old
when concept=land.react self.stage=old
say It's changed since I was young. Everything has. | I've walked most of it in my time. It's not got any flatter. | Aye. The land stays. We just pass over it.

rule e5_landr_devout
when concept=land.react self.piety=devout
say The Bright One made it well, then. | We should give thanks for it more than we do. | Every acre of it a gift.

rule e5_landr_restless
when concept=land.react self.drive=restless
say And past that? What's past all that? | One day I'll walk to the edge of it and see. | Makes me want to go and look.

# ===================================================================================== The turning year (talk.season)

rule e5_season_turn_spring
when concept=talk.season here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say Spring at last. You can smell it in the ground. | The light's come back. Have you noticed? | Buds on the old tree by the well. Every year I think it's dead.
then other: season.answer

rule e5_season_turn_summer
when concept=talk.season here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Long days. I could work till near midnight and still see. | High summer. Everything's buzzing. | The evenings go on forever this time of year.
then other: season.answer

rule e5_season_turn_autumn
when concept=talk.season here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Smoke and wet leaves. Autumn's here. | Nights are drawing in. | There's a bite in the morning air now.
then other: season.answer

rule e5_season_turn_winter
when concept=talk.season here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Dark before tea. I hate it. | Winter's dug its claws in. | Frost on the inside of the shutters this morning.
then other: season.answer

rule e5_season_old_bones
when concept=talk.season self.stage=old
cooldown 2d
say My bones told me the season was turning before the trees did. | Another turn of the year. They come quicker now. | I've seen a lot of these. Each one shorter.
then other: season.answer

# The answer, as the season falls on them (self answers):
rule e5_season_spring
when concept=season.answer here.season=spring
say #mud.capitalize# and lambs and everything green. | About time. I was sick of grey. | The ground's waking up. So am I, slowly.

rule e5_season_summer
when concept=season.answer here.season=summer
say Too hot to sleep and too light to try. | Make hay while the sun shines. Or sit in the shade. | Flies. That's summer to me. Flies.

rule e5_season_autumn
when concept=season.answer here.season=autumn
say Best time of year. Everything's ripe and nothing's frozen. | It's the leaves I love. And the cider. | Makes me sad, a bit. Everything getting ready to sleep.

rule e5_season_winter
when concept=season.answer here.season=winter
say Get by the fire and stay there. That's my plan. | Short days, long nights, and soup. | It'll turn. It always turns. Just not soon enough.

rule e5_season_farmer_spring
when concept=season.answer self.job=farmer here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say Ploughing, sowing, and praying for no late frost. | I've been behind the plough since dawn. My arms are rope. | Seed's in. Now we wait and fret.

rule e5_season_farmer_summer
when concept=season.answer self.job=farmer here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Haymaking. Every paw in the parish, while the weather holds. | Hoeing, weeding, and watching the sky for hail. | Good growing weather. Don't say it too loud.

rule e5_season_farmer_autumn
when concept=season.answer self.job=farmer here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Harvest. Don't talk to me till it's in. | Every sheaf counted twice. Then the tithe. Then what's left. | Barns full, backs broken. A good year.

rule e5_season_farmer_winter
when concept=season.answer self.job=farmer here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Mending fences and sharpening blades. The land's asleep; I'm not. | Muck-spreading and waiting. Mostly waiting. | Winter's when you find out if you stored enough.

rule e5_season_herder_spring
when concept=season.answer self.job=herder here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say Lambing. I've slept in the fold four nights running. | Twins, twins and a triplet last night. I'm dead on my feet. | Every lamb's a worry till it's standing.

rule e5_season_herder_summer
when concept=season.answer self.job=herder here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Shearing. My paws are greasy to the elbow with wool fat. | Up on the high grazing till the grass gives out. | Shorn sheep look so offended. Every year.

rule e5_season_herder_autumn
when concept=season.answer self.job=herder here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Bringing them down off the tops before the weather turns. | Counting, culling, and choosing who goes to market. Hard days. | The tups go in with the ewes. Lambs in spring, gods willing.

rule e5_season_herder_winter
when concept=season.answer self.job=herder here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Hay to the fold twice a day and break the ice on the troughs. | Lost one in a drift last week. Found her alive, the stubborn thing. | The beasts are hungry and so am I.

rule e5_season_fisher_summer
when concept=season.answer self.job=fisher|sailor here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Good fishing. Calm water, long light. | Out before dawn, back after dusk, and still light both ends. | Summer's when we earn the winter.

rule e5_season_fisher_winter
when concept=season.answer self.job=fisher|sailor here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Nets mending by the fire. Nobody goes out in this. | Ice on the ropes. My pads crack open. | The water doesn't care that it's winter. It just gets crueller.

rule e5_season_fisher_spring
when concept=season.answer self.job=fisher|sailor here.season=spring|autumn
cooldown 1d
say Tarring the boat and cursing the wind. | The fish don't know what season it is. They're either there or they're not. | Fair one day, foul the next. You go out and take your chances.

rule e5_season_woodcutter_winter
when concept=season.answer self.job=woodcutter here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Felling season. Sap's down, ground's hard, sledges run. | Best time to cut. Worst time to be cutting. | Every house wants wood at once. I'm worked to the bone.

rule e5_season_woodcutter_autumn
when concept=season.answer self.job=woodcutter here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Marking the trees for winter. Choose wrong and they rot. | Gathering kindling for half the town. | The woods are loud with acorns falling. Like rain.

rule e5_season_miner
when concept=season.answer self.job=miner
cooldown 1d
say What season? Down the pit it's always the same. Dark. | I go down in the dark and come up in the dark. The seasons happen to other folk. | Seasons are for those who see the sun.

rule e5_season_cook_autumn
when concept=season.answer self.job=cook|baker|innkeeper here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Pies. Apple, pear, blackberry. My kitchen smells like heaven. | Salting and pickling till my paws sting. | Game season. Everyone wants venison. Nobody wants to pay for it.

rule e5_season_cook_winter
when concept=season.answer self.job=cook|baker|innkeeper here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Stews. Every pot in the place, stew. | Everyone wants something hot and nobody wants to come out for it. | The salt meat's going fast. Hope it lasts to spring.

rule e5_season_cook_spring
when concept=season.answer self.job=cook|baker|innkeeper here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say First greens! Nettles, wild garlic. I could weep. | The stores are bare. Spring's the hungry gap. | New cheese, new milk. Everyone's sick of salt.

rule e5_season_cook_summer
when concept=season.answer self.job=cook|baker|innkeeper here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Too hot to stand by an oven, and I stand by one all day. | Cold meats and cheese and cider. Nobody wants hot food in this. | Strawberries! I've made tarts till I'm sick of the sight of them.

rule e5_season_young_winter
when concept=season.answer self.stage=young here.season=winter
say Snow! Well, maybe. I hope. | Snowball fights! I'm going to win. | I made a slide on the ice. Don't tell.

rule e5_season_young_summer
when concept=season.answer self.stage=young here.season=summer
say Swimming! And no lessons! | I caught a frog yesterday! | It's still light at bedtime. That's not fair, is it? It's still light!

rule e5_season_young_spring
when concept=season.answer self.stage=young here.season=spring|autumn
say Puddles! Big ones! | I found a bird's nest. I didn't touch it. Much. | I jumped in the biggest heap of leaves in the street. Or mud. Something, anyway.

rule e5_season_old_winter
when concept=season.answer self.stage=old here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Another one to get through. I've got through a few. | The cold's in my hips. It'll stay till spring. | Winter's the old wolf's enemy. I keep my fire high.

rule e5_season_old_spring
when concept=season.answer self.stage=old here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say Made it through another winter. I count that a win. | I love the spring more each year. You never know which is your last. | My mother used to say spring was a promise kept.

rule e5_season_priest
when concept=season.answer self.job=priest
cooldown 1d
say Every season has its feast and its fast. We keep them all. | The Bright One turns the year. We just follow. | I bless the plough in spring and the sheaves in autumn. The rest is the same.

rule e5_season_carter
when concept=season.answer self.job=carter
cooldown 1d
say Seasons are just which kind of #mud#. | Summer's dust, autumn's #mud#, winter's ice, spring's #mud# again. | Any season the roads are open's a good one.

rule e5_season_frosthollow_winter
when concept=season.answer here.region=frosthollow here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Snow to the eaves. We dig tunnels to the neighbours. | Winter up here isn't a season. It's a siege. | The smelter's the warmest place in town. Half of us find excuses to stand by it.

rule e5_season_frosthollow_summer
when concept=season.answer here.region=frosthollow here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Blink and it's gone. I'm not blinking. | The goats are up on the high meadows and the whole town smells of hay. | Summer up here is when the snow's only on the peaks.

rule e5_season_southmeadow_summer
when concept=season.answer here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer
cooldown 1d
say Dust on everything. Dust in the bread. | The Sunfields are gold. Pray for one good rain before harvest. | Too hot to work past noon. We sleep under the hedges.

rule e5_season_southmeadow_autumn
when concept=season.answer here.region=southmeadow here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Apple-picking. Ladders in every tree in the Vale. | Grain in, apples in, grapes in. Then we sleep for a month. | Wasps in everything. Drunk on windfalls, the lot of them.

rule e5_season_coldwater_winter
when concept=season.answer here.region=coldwater here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Ice on the lake and ice on the jetty. I've gone over twice this week. | The boats are hauled up and turned over. Nothing to do but mend nets. | Ice fishing, and lying about how thick it is.

rule e5_season_mirebridge_autumn
when concept=season.answer here.region=mirebridge here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say The geese are in off the delta. The whole fen honks all night. | Everything's damp. The bread, the bedding, your own tail. | The reedcutters are out. You hear the sickles all over the fen.

rule e5_season_mirebridge_winter
when concept=season.answer here.region=mirebridge here.season=winter
cooldown 1d
say Peat on the fire, and coughing. That's a fen winter. | The channels skin over with ice, too thin to walk and too thick to row. | Damp gets into your bones out here. Into the bread, too.

rule e5_season_larkfield_spring
when concept=season.answer here.region=larkfield here.season=spring
cooldown 1d
say Spring on the downs. The wind's soft for once. | The Lark's full and fast and the mill's going day and night. | Cowslips all over the high grazing. The sheep eat them like sweets.

rule e5_season_ridgemere_autumn
when concept=season.answer here.region=ridgemere here.season=autumn|winter
cooldown 1d
say Rain. Same as summer, only colder. | The wind comes up the Sound and straight through your coat. | Wet dark at both ends of the day. I live by lamplight.

rule e5_season_oakhaven_autumn
when concept=season.answer here.region=oakhaven here.season=autumn
cooldown 1d
say Mast year. Acorns everywhere. The pigs are in heaven. | Leaves to the ankles on every path. You can hear folk coming a mile off. | Mushrooms, nuts, honey. The woods are giving everything away.
